# -*- mode:org;coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: autolisp-spec: incorporate the engine facts from the alpm verification campaign
#+AUTHOR: Pascal J. Bourguignon
#+DATE: 2026-08-09

The alpm project (gitlab.com/ogamita/alpm) ran a portability probe
suite (tests/verification/verification.lsp, greppable ALPM-VERIFY
protocol) on FOUR engine/platform combinations via CI:

- clautolisp 1.8.21 (macOS)
- BricsCAD V26 macOS (LispEx 26.0, fr_FR) -- job verify:bricscad:macos
- BricsCAD V25 Windows (LispEx 25.0, fr)  -- job verify:bricscad:windows
- AutoCAD 2022 Windows (Visual LISP, fr)  -- job verify:autocad:windows

plus a documentation pass over the VLISP project-file formats and
DEScoder (alpm documentation/vlisp-project-files.org). The following
facts are direct probe observations; several contradict or refine
what the autolisp-spec draft currently says (many BricsCAD entries
are marked "presumed compatible pending Phase 4 verification" --
here is Phase-4-grade data for a batch of them).

* Per-entry updates

** LOAD
Explicit non-.lsp extensions load as LISP source on ALL engines:
(load "foo.alpm") evaluates the file on clautolisp, BricsCAD
V25/V26, AutoCAD 2022 (probe V-1). The entry should state: when an
extension is given explicitly, the file is read as source whatever
the extension (binary formats are recognized by content/extension,
not by rejecting others).

** VL-FILE-SYSTIME
Three different shapes for the same instant (Sun 2026-08-09):
- AutoCAD 2022 runtime: (2026 8 0 9 12 21 8 1) -- 8 elements WITH
  milliseconds, day-of-week Sunday=0. This CONTRADICTS Autodesk's
  own documentation example (2003-09-10 Wednesday documented as
  day-of-week 4, implying Sunday=1).
- BricsCAD V25/V26: (2026 8 7 9 ...) -- 8 elements, Sunday=7.
- clautolisp 1.8.21: (2026 8 0 9 ...) -- 7 elements, NO
  milliseconds, Sunday=0.
The entry should document the three conventions, the doc-vs-runtime
divergence on AutoCAD, and advise portable use of positions
1,2,4,5,6,7 only. (clautolisp fix tracked separately in
vl-file-systime-parity.issue.)

** WRITE-CHAR / READ-CHAR
Asymmetric and PLATFORM-dependent (probe V-10, byte 233):
- write-char emits the CHARACTER in the platform encoding: one byte
  233 on clautolisp and on both Windows CADs; the UTF-8 pair C3 A9
  on BricsCAD V26 macOS.
- read-char returns RAW BYTES one at a time on every engine
  (BricsCAD macOS reads back (195 169), not 233).
The entries should state: write-char cannot portably emit arbitrary
bytes; read-char is byte-level; the pair is only a round-trip on
single-byte platforms.

** VL-DOC-EXPORT (and family) -- upgrade BricsCAD from "presumed"
The WHOLE family is present as SUBRs on AutoCAD 2022 AND BricsCAD
V25/V26 (probe V-9 inventory): vl-doc-export, vl-doc-import,
vl-doc-ref, vl-doc-set, vl-arx-import, vl-list-exported-functions,
vl-list-loaded-vlx, vl-vlx-loaded-p, vl-unload-vlx, vl-acad-defun,
vl-acad-undefun, vl-bb-ref, vl-bb-set.
(vl-doc-export 'f) executed in the DOCUMENT namespace (plain source
load) is an inert success everywhere: returns T on both CADs, the
symbol on clautolisp (probe V-7). Entries currently reading
"BricsCAD V26: presumed compatible" can be marked verified; the
multidocument-analysis correction is tracked in
vl-doc-namespace-family-bricscad.issue.

** DEFUN-Q / DEFUN-Q-LIST-REF
Verified behaviorally on all four combinations: defun-q defines a
callable and defun-q-list-ref returns the list form ((X) X)
(probe V-11). BricsCAD entries can move from presumed to verified.

** VL-DIRECTORY-FILES
Autodesk signature verified on all engines: directories argument
-1 = directories only, 1 = files only (probe V-6).

** FINDFILE
Absolute forward-slash paths accepted on all engines; the CADs
return the found path with BACKSLASHES (probe V-5) -- worth a note
that results must be normalized before comparison.

* Chapter 22 (Packaging, Compilation, and Namespaces) additions

** DEScoder command line (v2.10, shipped with BricsCAD V25)
From the DEScoder GUI help (no web documentation exists):
  DESCoder.exe [-namespace] [-keep] [-nocompress] [-dialog] [SourceLisp]
  DESCoder.exe [-keep] [-nocompress] [-dialog] [-project ProjectFile]
  DESCoder.exe [-keep] [-nocompress] [-dialog] ProjectFile.prv
  DESCoder.exe [-keep] [-nocompress] [-dialog] ProjectFile.prj
- SourceLisp accepts wildcards (batch mode); any extension allowed.
- -namespace = the VLX-NameSpace opt-in (recent DEScoder only);
  listed only in the single-file form -- project mode presumably
  inherits the .prv (:separate-namespace) entry (hypothesis).
- -keep = the GUI's "Keep the Intermediate Files".
- ProjectFile must be .prj or .prv: DEScoder CONSUMES the VLISP
  project formats -- they are load-bearing on BricsCAD.
- Without usable arguments DEScoder opens its GUI and waits
  (observed): automation must bound the process.

** VLX NameSpace compatibility (Bricsys DevRef)
DES data format and options storage are backward+forward
compatible; a namespace-ENABLED .des on pre-V18.2 engines loads
with "no NameSpaces available" -- isolation silently degrades to
global visibility. Consequence worth normative text: code must not
rely on namespace hiding for correctness.

** .prj / .prv file formats
No official specification exists; the formats are documented from
four real samples (schms export.prj/prv, schmsplus.prj/prv,
autolisp-misc.prj) plus the Make Application wizard GUI mapping in
alpm's documentation/vlisp-project-files.org. Highlights the spec
should carry (or cite):
- .prj = VLISP-PROJECT-LIST property list; KEY CASE IS UNSTABLE
  across writers (:NAME 2024 vs :name 2025 from the same product);
  :OWN-LIST entries appear with and without the .lsp extension;
  :PROJECT-KEYS :BUILD (:standard) vs (:OPTIMIZE :LOCALIZE :link)
  maps to the GUI Standard vs Optimize-and-Link, i.e. to the
  vlisp-compile modes st vs lsm/lsa.
- .prv = PRV-DEF ASSOCIATION list (different shape!): :target,
  :active-x, :separate-namespace, :protected, :load-file-list
  (tags = the wizard's Program File Types; :prj observed),
  :require-file-list (:DCL observed, :DVB expected),
  :ob-directory, :tmp-directory, :optimization (st/lsm/lsa).
- Empty (:separate-namespace) means OFF (two independent proofs:
  the schms VLXs' functions are visible to runtime-loaded files,
  and the schms sources contain zero vl-doc-export).
- (:active-x . T) is stored even when the GUI gates the checkbox on
  Separate Namespace -- do not infer namespace mode from it.

** VLISP-COMPILE
The existing entry's divergence note can be enriched with the GUI
mapping: mode st = wizard "Standard" = .prj :BUILD (:standard);
lsm/lsa = "Optimize and Link" = :BUILD (:OPTIMIZE :LOCALIZE :link);
.prv (:optimization . st|lsm|lsa).

